"Importance-Guided Basis Selection for Low-Rank Decomposition of Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2605.01627v1)\nciteturn39277view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2605.01627v1\",\"lineno\":830}); Total lines: 866\nL612: Since $\\{\\mathbf{u}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n}$ is a set of orthonormal bases, $\\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{\\ell}=0$ for $k\\neq\\ell$ and $\\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{k}=1$, which yields\nL613: \nL614:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{H}^{2}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\lambda_{k}^{2}\\mathbf{u}_{k}\\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\\top}.$  |\nL617:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Tr}(\\mathbf{H}^{2})=\\mathrm{Tr}\\!\\left(\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\lambda_{k}^{2}\\mathbf{u}_{k}\\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\\top}\\right)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\lambda_{k}^{2}\\mathrm{Tr}(\\mathbf{u}_{k}\\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\\top})=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\lambda_{k}^{2}\\mathrm{Tr}(\\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{k})=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\lambda_{k}^{2}.$  |\nL618: \nL619: Combining the above yields\nL620:  | $$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\big(\\mathrm{D}^{(s)}(\\mathbf{H})_{i}\\big)\\leq\\frac{1}{s}\\mathrm{Tr}(\\mathbf{H}^{2})=\\frac{1}{s}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\lambda_{k}^{2}.$$  |  | (3)\nL621: \nL622: Under the spectral power-law assumption (Assumption cite64†8 ), $|\\lambda_{k}|\\leq|\\lambda_{1}|k^{-\\alpha}$, and hence $\\lambda_{k}^{2}\\leq|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}k^{-2\\alpha}$. Substituting into (cite96†3 ) gives\nL623:  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\big(\\mathrm{D}^{(s)}(\\mathbf{H})_{i}\\big)\\leq\\frac{1}{s}|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{k^{2\\alpha}}=\\frac{1}{s}|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}H_{n,2\\alpha}\\leq\\frac{1}{s}|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}\\zeta(2\\alpha).$  |\nL624: \nL625: As $\\alpha>\\tfrac{1}{2}$, the series converges as $n\\to\\infty$. This bound for the total variance is independent of parameter dimensionality $n$.\nL626: \nL627: ∎\nL628: ### A.4 Probabilistic Guarantee for Hessian Diagonal Estimation\nL629: \nL630: We strengthen the variance analysis of the Hutchinson diagonal estimator by deriving a probabilistic bound for the estimation error. Our result, which is presented in Theorem cite67†11 , is connected to the spectral structure of the Hessian. The proof of Theorem cite67†11 uses the following Lemma:\nL631: ###### Lemma 15.\nL632: \nL633: For any $\\varepsilon>0$, $0<\\delta<1$, and matrix $\\mathbf{A}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}$ with $\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{A})\\neq\\mathbf{0}$, the Rademacher diagonal estimator $\\mathrm{D}^{(s)}(\\mathbf{A})$ is an $(\\varepsilon,\\delta)$-approximator if the number of samples $s$ satisfies\nL634: \nL635:  | $$s>2\\left(\\frac{\\|\\mathbf{A}\\|_{F}^{2}-\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{A})\\|_{2}^{2}}{\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{A})\\|_{2}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right).$$  |\nL636: ###### Proof.\nL637: \nL638: cite97†Baston and Nakatsukasa (2022) proposed the lemma, which we restate here. ∎\nL639: \nL640: See cite67†11 L641: ###### Proof.\nL642: \nL643: We begin with the decomposition\nL644: \nL645:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{H}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}.$  |\nL646: \nL647: By definition of the Frobenius norm,\nL648: \nL649:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\mathbf{H}\\|_{F}^{2}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{H}^{\\top}).$  |\nL650: \nL651: Expanding $\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{H}^{\\top}$, we obtain\nL652:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{H}^{\\top}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\right)\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda_{j}\\mathbf{u}_{j}\\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\\top}\\right)^{\\top}=\\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}\\lambda_{j}\\,\\mathbf{u}_{i}(\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{v}_{j})\\mathbf{u}_{j}^{\\top}.$  |\nL653: Since $\\{\\mathbf{v}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ is a set of orthonormal bases, $\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{v}_{j}=0$ for $i\\neq j$ and $\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{v}_{i}=1$, which yields\nL654: \nL655:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{H}^{\\top}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}^{2}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}.$  |\nL656: \nL657: Therefore,\nL658:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\mathbf{H}\\|_{F}^{2}=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{H}^{\\top})=\\operatorname{Tr}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}^{2}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}\\right)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}^{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top})=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}^{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}^{2}.$  |\nL659: \nL660: Under the power-law decay condition,\nL661:  | $\\displaystyle|\\lambda_{i}|$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\frac{|\\lambda_{1}|}{i^{\\alpha}},$  |\nL662: \nL663: Hence,\nL664: \nL665:  | $\\displaystyle\\lambda_{i}^{2}$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\frac{|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}}{i^{2\\alpha}}.$  |\nL666: \nL667: Consequently,\nL668: \nL669:  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}^{2}\\leq|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{i^{2\\alpha}}=|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}H_{n,2\\alpha}.$  |\nL670: \nL671: Therefore,\nL672: \nL673:  | $$\\|\\mathbf{H}\\|_{F}^{2}\\;\\leq\\;|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}\\,H_{n,2\\alpha}$$  |  | (4)\nL674: \nL675: Next, we bound the diagonal term. By Cauchy–Schwarz,\nL676:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}H_{ii}^{2}\\geq\\frac{1}{n}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}H_{ii}\\right)^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})^{2}.$  |\nL677: \nL678: Hence,\nL679: \nL680:  | $$\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}\\;\\geq\\;\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})^{2}.$$  |  | (5)\nL681: \nL682: Combining Eq. (cite98†4 ) and Eq. (cite99†5 ),\nL683:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\|\\mathbf{H}\\|_{F}^{2}-\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}}{\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\|\\mathbf{H}\\|_{F}^{2}}{\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}}-1$  |\nL684:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\frac{|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}H_{n,2\\alpha}}{\\tfrac{1}{n}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})^{2}}-1.$  |\nL685: \nL686: From Lemma cite100†15 ,\nL687:  | $\\displaystyle\\Pr\\!\\left(\\left\\|\\mathrm{D}^{(s)}(\\mathbf{H})-\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\right\\|_{2}\\leq\\varepsilon\\left\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\right\\|_{2}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq 1-\\delta$  |\nL688: \nL689: provided that\nL690: \nL691:  | $\\displaystyle s$  | $\\displaystyle>2\\left(\\frac{\\|\\mathbf{H}\\|_{F}^{2}-\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}}{\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right).$  |\nL692: \nL693: Therefore, if\nL694:  | $\\displaystyle s$  | $\\displaystyle>\\left(\\frac{|\\lambda_{1}|^{2}H_{n,2\\alpha}}{\\tfrac{1}{n}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})^{2}}-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right)\\;\\geq\\;\\left(\\frac{\\|\\mathbf{H}\\|_{F}^{2}-\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}}{\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\|_{2}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right),$  |\nL695: \nL696: we can guarantee that\nL697:  | $\\displaystyle\\Pr\\!\\left(\\left\\|\\mathrm{D}^{(s)}-\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\right\\|_{2}\\leq\\varepsilon\\left\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{H})\\right\\|_{2}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq 1-\\delta.$  |\nL698: \nL699: ∎\nL700: #### A.4.1 Probabilistic Bound for Positive semidefinite Hessians\nL701: When the Hessian is positive semidefinite, the general probabilistic bound derived in Theorem cite67†11 can be simplified. In this setting, the trace of the Hessian admits a direct lower bound in terms of the leading eigenvalue, yielding a simplified condition on the number of samples required. Corollary cite68†12 provides an explicit $(\\varepsilon,\\delta)$ probabilistic bound on the sample complexity for Hessian-diagonal estimation in the positive semidefinite case.\nL702: \nL703: See cite68†12 L704: ###### Proof.\nL705: \nL706: As $\\mathbf{H}$ is positive semidefinite, $\\lambda_{i}\\geq 0$, $i=1,\\ldots,n$. Further because $\\lambda_{i}>0$,\nL707: \nL708:  | $$\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lambda_{i}\\geq\\lambda_{1}>0.$$  |\nL709: \nL710: Based on Theorem cite67†11 , the Rademacher diagonal estimator $\\mathrm{D}^{(s)}$ is a relative $(\\varepsilon,\\delta)$-approximator if\nL711:  | $$s>2\\left(\\frac{|\\lambda_{1}^{2}|H_{n,2\\alpha}}{\\tfrac{1}{n}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})^{2}}-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right).$$  |\nL712: \nL713: As $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})\\geq\\lambda_{1}$,\nL714:  | $\\displaystyle 2\\left(\\frac{|\\lambda_{1}^{2}|H_{n,2\\alpha}}{\\tfrac{1}{n}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\mathbf{H})^{2}}-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right)$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq 2\\left(\\frac{|\\lambda_{1}^{2}|H_{n,2\\alpha}}{\\tfrac{1}{n}|\\lambda_{1}^{2}|}-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right)$  |\nL715:  |  | $\\displaystyle=2\\left(n\\,H_{n,2\\alpha}-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right)$  |\nL716:  |  | $\\displaystyle<2\\left(n\\,\\zeta(2\\alpha)-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right).$  |\nL717: Therefore, $\\mathrm{D}^{(s)}$ is a relative $(\\varepsilon,\\delta)$-approximator if $s>2\\left(n\\,\\zeta(2\\alpha)-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(2n/\\delta)}{\\varepsilon^{2}}\\right).$ ∎\nL718: ### A.5 Non-negativity of the Hessian diagonal of the output layer (lm_head)\nL719: \nL720: For the output layer, Theorem cite70†13 indicates that the Hessian diagonal of the output layer is non-negative.\nL721: \nL722: See cite70†13 L723: ###### Proof.\nL724: \nL725: We proceed by direct differentiation. For\nL726: \nL727:  | $$p_{k}=\\frac{e^{z_{k}}}{\\sum_{j}e^{z_{j}}},$$  |\nL728: \nL729: Then\nL730: \nL731:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial p_{k}}{\\partial z_{j}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}\\displaystyle\\frac{e^{z_{k}}}{\\sum_{j}e^{z_{j}}}-\\frac{e^{2z_{k}}}{\\left(\\sum_{j}e^{z_{j}}\\right)^{2}}=p_{k}(1-p_{k}),&k=j,\\\\[8.0pt]\nL732: \\displaystyle-\\frac{e^{z_{k}}e^{z_{j}}}{\\left(\\sum_{j}e^{z_{j}}\\right)^{2}}=-p_{k}p_{j},&k\\neq j.\\end{cases}$  |\nL733: \nL734: Consequently,\nL735:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{p}}{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}.$  |\nL736: \nL737: where $\\mathbf{p}=[p_{1},\\ldots,p_{n}]^{\\top}$ and $\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})$ constructs a diagonal matrix with $\\mathbf{p}$ as its diagonal. The loss can be written as\nL738: \nL739:  | $\\displaystyle\\ell$  | $\\displaystyle=-\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\hat{p}_{k}\\log p_{k}.$  |\nL740: \nL741: Its derivative with respect to $p_{k}$ is\nL742:  | $$\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial p_{k}}=-\\frac{\\hat{p}_{k}}{p_{k}}.$$  |\nL743: \nL744: Therefore,\nL745: \nL746:  | $$\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\mathbf{p}}=-(\\mathbf{\\hat{p}}\\oslash\\mathbf{p})^{\\top}.$$  |\nL747: \nL748: The derivative of $\\mathbf{z}$ with respect to $\\sigma_{i}$ is\nL749: \nL750:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x}.$  |\nL751: \nL752: Applying the chain rule,\nL753:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\mathbf{p}}\\,\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{p}}{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}\\,\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}$  |\nL754:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-(\\mathbf{\\hat{p}}\\oslash\\mathbf{p})^{\\top}(\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top})(\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x})$  |\nL755:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-\\big(\\mathbf{\\hat{p}}^{\\top}-\\|\\mathbf{\\hat{p}}\\|_{1}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\big)(\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x}).$  |\nL756: As $\\|\\hat{\\mathbf{p}}\\|_{1}=1$, this simplifies to\nL757: \nL758:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}$  | $\\displaystyle=-(\\mathbf{\\hat{p}}^{\\top}-\\mathbf{p}^{\\top})(\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x}).$  |\nL759: \nL760: Differentiating once more,\nL761:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial^{2}\\ell}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}^{2}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}$  |\nL762:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\mathbf{p}}\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{p}}{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{z}}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}}$  |\nL763:  |  | $\\displaystyle=(\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x})^{\\top}(\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top})(\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x}).$  |\nL764: Factoring out the scalar term $(\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x})^{2}$, we obtain\nL765: \nL766:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial^{2}\\ell}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}^{2}}$  | $\\displaystyle=(\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x})^{2}\\,\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}(\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top})\\mathbf{u}_{i}.$  |\nL767: \nL768: Next,\nL769:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}(\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top})\\mathbf{u}_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})\\mathbf{u}_{i}-\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i}$  |\nL770:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k}p_{k}\\,u_{ik}^{2}-(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})^{2}$  |\nL771:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k}p_{k}\\,u_{ik}^{2}-2(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})^{2}+(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})^{2}$  |\nL772:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k}p_{k}\\,u_{ik}^{2}-2\\sum_{k}p_{k}u_{ik}(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})+(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})^{2}.$  |\nL773: Further, because $\\sum\\limits_{k}p_{k}=1$,\nL774:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}(\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top})\\mathbf{u}_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k}p_{k}\\,u_{ik}^{2}-2\\sum_{k}p_{k}u_{ik}(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})+\\sum_{k}p_{k}(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})^{2}$  |\nL775:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k}p_{k}\\Big[u_{ik}^{2}-2u_{ik}(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})+(\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i})^{2}\\Big]$  |\nL776:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k}p_{k}\\big(u_{ik}-\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\big)^{2}$  |\nL777:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\big(\\mathbf{u}_{i}-\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\big)^{2}.$  |\nL778: Because $\\mathbf{p}\\geq 0$ and $\\big(\\mathbf{u}_{i}-\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\big)^{2}\\geq 0$, it follows that\nL779: \nL780:  | $$\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}(\\mathrm{Diag}(\\mathbf{p})-\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^{\\top})\\mathbf{u}_{i}=\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\big(\\mathbf{u}_{i}-\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\big)^{2}\\geq 0.$$  |\nL781: \nL782: Therefore,\nL783:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial^{2}\\ell}{\\partial\\sigma_{i}^{2}}$  | $\\displaystyle=(\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x})^{2}\\,\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\big(\\mathbf{u}_{i}-\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\big)^{2}\\;\\geq\\;0$  |\nL784: \nL785: ∎\nL786: ## Appendix B BSI Practical Design Choices\nL787: \nL788: This appendix section describes the practical design choices needed for a numerically stable and computationally efficient implementation of BSI in large-scale models. Specifically, it addresses (i) the selection of perturbation intensity for reliable Hessian-diagonal estimation under finite precision, and (ii) the use of a candidate pool to limit the computational cost of Hessian estimation during pruning.\nL789: ### B.1 Choosing Perturbation Intensity\nL790: \nL791: We consider the floating-point representation of singular value $s_{i}$,\nL792: \nL793:  | $\\displaystyle s_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=(-1)^{\\text{sign}}\\cdot(1+\\text{fraction})\\cdot 2^{h},$  |\nL794: \nL795: where the fraction is represented using $f$ bits and $s_{i}$ denotes the singular value that we perturb when computing the Hessian estimate in Theorem cite55†5 . The rounding error incurs when perturbing $s_{i}$ by $\\epsilon$ satisfies\nL796:  | $$\\mathrm{Error}[s_{i}+\\epsilon]\\leq\\frac{1}{2}\\,2^{h-f}.$$  |  | (6)\nL797: \nL798: As $2^{h}<|s_{i}|<2^{h+1},$ we have\nL799: \nL800:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{2}|s_{i}|$  | $\\displaystyle<2^{h}<|s_{i}|.$  |\nL801: \nL802: Substituting this bound in Eq. (cite101†6 ) yields\nL803: \nL804:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Error}[s_{i}+\\epsilon]$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\frac{1}{2}\\,2^{-f}\\,|s_{i}|.$  |\nL805: \nL806: Let $\\alpha$ denote the relative error tolerance:\nL807: \nL808:  | $$\\frac{\\mathrm{Error}(s_{i}+\\epsilon)}{\\epsilon}\\leq\\alpha.$$  |  | (7)\nL809: We need to ensure the largest possible rounding error still satisfies Eq. (cite102†7 ). Therefore,\nL810: \nL811:  | $$\\frac{\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,2^{-f}\\,|s_{i}|}{\\epsilon}\\leq\\alpha.$$  |\nL812: \nL813: Hence\nL814: \nL815:  | $\\displaystyle\\epsilon$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq\\frac{1}{2\\alpha}\\,2^{-f}\\,|s_{i}|$  |\nL816:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{\\alpha}\\,2^{-(f+1)}\\,|s_{i}|.$  |\nL817: At the same time, the perturbation intensity must remain sufficiently small to avoid contamination induced by higher order terms. We therefore bound $\\epsilon$ by $\\epsilon_{max}<1$ and choose:\nL818: \nL819:  | $$\\epsilon\\;=\\;\\min\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{\\alpha}\\,2^{-(f+1)}\\,s_{\\max},\\;\\epsilon_{max}\\right),$$  |\nL820: where $s_{\\max}$ denotes the largest singular value across all layers of the model and $\\epsilon_{\\max}$ ensures the perturbation remains small enough for reliable estimation. This setting yields a perturbation intensity suitable for all singular values in the model. Using a uniform perturbation intensity reduces estimation variance by alleviating the off-diagonal noise induced by the large singular values.\nL821: ### B.2 Candidate Pool for Hessian Estimate\nL822: Estimating the Hessian diagonal for all singular values at every pruning step is computationally expensive for large models. To address this, we restrict Hessian estimation to a dynamically constructed candidate pool consisting of bases that are likely to be dropped at each pruning round. Formally, let $\\mathcal{A}$ denote the set of currently active bases, and let $T$ be the total number of pruning rounds.\nL823: We first identify the set of most significant bases $\\mathcal{K}\\subset\\mathcal{A}$ as the minimal subset satisfying:\nL824:  | $$\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{K}}\\sigma_{i}\\geq\\rho_{\\mathrm{cand}}\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{A}}\\sigma_{j},\\qquad\\rho_{\\mathrm{cand}}=(\\texttt{KeepRatio})^{\\gamma/T},$$  |  | (8)\nL825: \nL826: where KeepRatio is the final target retention ratio of the model parameters, and $\\gamma>0$ controls the size of the candidate pool. $\\rho_{\\mathrm{cand}}$ is the cumulative sum threshold that we use to form the candidate pool. At a given pruning round, the candidate pool $\\mathcal{C}$ is defined as the set of remaining bases:\nL827:  | $$\\mathcal{C}=\\mathcal{A}\\setminus\\mathcal{K}$$  |\nL828: \nL829: Hessian diagonals are estimated only for singular values corresponding to the bases in the candidate pool. The size of this pool is given by the cardinality $|\\mathcal{C}|=|\\mathcal{A}|-|\\mathcal{K}|$, where $|\\mathcal{K}|$ is the number of bases required to satisfy the cumulative sum threshold.\nL830: ## Appendix C BSI Application\nL831: ### C.1 Evaluation of BSI on Mathematical Reasoning\nL832: \nL833: This section presents results on the performance of models compressed using BSI on mathematical reasoning tasks. We report performance under varying compression ratios. As Table cite103†1 shows, BSI outperforms state-of-the-art low-rank compression baselines at deep compression.\nL834: Table 1: Accuracy and model size of Llama 2-7B compressed with various low-rank decomposition algorithms on the mathematical reasoning tasks.\nL835: SVD  | Model Size (Billion)  | 6.74  | 5.03  | 3.18  | 1.73  | 1.11  | 0.56\nL836: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL837: GSM8K Acc (%)  | 66.41  | 63.00  | 60.96  | 53.90  | 32.90  | 11.90\nL838: MATH Acc (%)  | 20.56  | 18.30  | 17.38  | 13.74  | 5.34  | 2.76\nL839: FWSVD  | Model Size (Billion)  | 6.74  | 4.79  | 2.95  | 1.54  | 0.96  | 0.47\nL840: GSM8K Acc (%)  | 66.41  | 62.70  | 62.55  | 56.48  | 1.52  | 1.90\nL841: MATH Acc (%)  | 20.56  | 19.18  | 17.62  | 14.20  | 1.76  | 1.48\nL842: ASVD  | Model Size (Billion)  | 6.74  | 4.09  | 3.43  | 2.77  | 1.45  | 0.79\nL843: GSM8K Acc (%)  | 66.41  | 61.94  | 60.88  | 59.74  | 50.49  | 29.11\nL844: MATH Acc (%)  | 20.56  | 18.74  | 18.72  | 17.30  | 12.62  | 5.18\nL845: BASEL  | Model Size (Billion)  | 6.74  | 5.11  | 3.27  | 1.82  | 1.20  | 0.41\nL846: GSM8K Acc (%)  | 66.41  | 62.55  | 61.03  | 56.86  | 50.34  | 7.58\nL847: MATH Acc (%)  | 20.56  | 19.50  | 18.62  | 16.20  | 13.18  | 2.72\nL848: BSI  | Model Size (Billion)  | 6.74  | 4.52  | 2.85  | 1.59  | 0.51  | 0.34\nL849: GSM8K Acc (%)  | 66.41  | 62.85  | 60.35  | 56.71  | 35.03  | 17.66\nL850: MATH Acc (%)  | 20.56  | 19.44  | 18.08  | 15.72  | 7.78  | 4.10\nL851: Experimental support, please cite104†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite105†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL852: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL853: \nL854: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL855: \nL856:   * Click the \"Report Issue